#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:2
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &kmplot;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:4
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kmplot; deals with several different types of functions, which can be "
"written in function form or as an equation:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:7
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Cartesians plots can either be written as &eg; <quote>y = x^2</quote>, where "
"x has to be used as the variable; or as &eg; <quote>f(a) = a^2</quote>, "
"where the name of the variable is arbitrary."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:8
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Parametric plots are similar to Cartesian plots. The x and y coordinates can "
"be entered as equations in t, &eg; <quote>x = sin(t)</quote>, <quote>y = cos"
"(t)</quote>, or as functions, &eg; <quote>f_x(s) = sin(s)</quote>, <quote>f_y"
"(s) = cos(s)</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:9
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Polar plots are also similar to Cartesian plots. They can be either be "
"entered as an equation in &thgr;, &eg; <quote>r = &thgr;</quote>, or as a "
"function, e.g. <quote>f(x) = x</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:10
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For implicit plots, the name of the function is entered separetely from the "
"expression relating the x and y coordinates. If the x and y variables are "
"specified via the function name (by entering &eg;<quote>f(a,b)</quote> as "
"the function name), then these variables will be used. Otherwise, the "
"letters x and y will be used for the variables."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:11
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Explicit differential plots are differential equations whereby the highest "
"derivatve is given in terms of the lower derivatives. Differentiatation is "
"denoted by a prime ('). In function form, the equation will look like "
"<quote>f''(x) = f' &minus; f</quote>. In equation form, it will look like "
"<quote>y'' = y' &minus; y</quote>. Note that in both cases, the <quote>(x)</"
"quote> part is not added to the lower order differential terms (so you would "
"enter <quote>f'(x) = &minus;f</quote> and not <quote>f'(x) = &minus;f(x)</"
"quote>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:14
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"All the equation entry boxes come with a button on the right. Clicking this "
"invokes the advanced <guilabel>Equation Editor</guilabel> dialog, which "
"provides:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A variety of mathematical symbols that can be used in equations, but aren't "
"found on normal keyboards."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:21
#, no-c-format
msgid "The list of user constants and a button for editing them."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:24
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The list of predefined functions. Note that if you have text already "
"selected, it will be used as the function argument when a function is "
"inserted. For example, if <quote>1 + x</quote> is selected in the equation "
"<quote>y = 1 + x</quote>, and the sine function is chosen, then the equation "
"will become <quote> y = sin(1+x)</quote>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: using.docbook:31
#, no-c-format
msgid "Here is a screenshot of the &kmplot; welcome window"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: using.docbook:37
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot"
msgstr "Ecran"

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:43
#, no-c-format
msgid "Function Types"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:46
#, no-c-format
msgid "Cartesian Functions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:47
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To enter an explicit function (&ie;, a function in the form y=f(x)) into "
"&kmplot;, just enter it in the following form: "
"<screen><userinput><replaceable>f</replaceable>(<replaceable>x</"
"replaceable>) = <replaceable>expression</replaceable></userinput></screen> "
"where:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:52
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<replaceable>f</replaceable> is the name of the function, and can be any "
"string of letters and numbers."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:57
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<replaceable>x</replaceable> is the x-coordinate, to be used in the "
"expression following the equals sign. It is a dummy variable, so you can use "
"any variable name you like to achieve the same effect."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:64
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<replaceable>expression</replaceable> is the expression to be plotted, given "
"in the appropriate syntax for &kmplot;. See <xref linkend=\"math-syntax\"/>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:74
#, no-c-format
msgid "Parametric Functions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Parametric functions are those in which the x and y coordinates are defined "
"by separate functions of another variable, often called t. To enter a "
"parametric function in &kmplot;, follow the procedure as for a Cartesian "
"function for each of the x and y functions. As with Cartesian functions, you "
"may use any variable name you wish for the parameter."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As an example, suppose you want to draw a circle, which has parametric "
"equations x = sin(t), y = cos(t). After creating a parametric plot, enter "
"the appropriate equations in the x and y boxes, &ie;, <guilabel>xcircle(t) = "
"</guilabel><userinput>sin(t)</userinput> and <guilabel>ycircle(t) = </"
"guilabel><userinput>cos(t)</userinput>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:85 using.docbook:136
#, no-c-format
msgid "You can set some further options for the plot in the function editor:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: using.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guilabel>Min</guilabel>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: using.docbook:89
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guilabel>Max</guilabel>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:91
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"These options control the range of the parameter t for which the function is "
"plotted."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:99
#, no-c-format
msgid "Functions in Polar Coordinates"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Polar coordinates represent a point by its distance from the origin (usually "
"called r), and the angle a line from the origin to the point makes with the "
"x-axis (usually represented by &thgr; the Greek letter theta). To enter "
"functions in polar coordinates, create a new Polar Plot from the "
"<guilabel>Create New Plot</guilabel> button. In the definition box, complete "
"the function definition, including the name of the theta variable you want "
"to use, &eg;, to draw the Archimedes' spiral r = &thgr;, enter: "
"<screen><userinput>r(theta) = theta</userinput></screen> so that the whole "
"line reads <quote>r(theta) = theta</quote>. Note that you can use any name "
"for the theta variable, so <quote>r(foo) = foo</quote> will produce exactly "
"the same output."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:116
#, no-c-format
msgid "Implicit Functions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:118
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"An implicit expression relates the x and y coordinates as an equality. To "
"create a circle, for example, create a new Implicit Plot from the "
"<guilabel>Create New Plot</guilabel> button. Then, enter into the equation "
"box (below the function name box) the following:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: using.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid "<userinput>x^2 + y^2 = 25</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:124
#, no-c-format
msgid "Differential Functions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:126
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kmplot; can plot explicit differential equations. These are equations of "
"the form y<superscript>(n)</superscript> = F(x,y',y'',...,y<superscript>"
"(n&minus;1)</superscript>), where y<superscript>k</superscript> is the "
"k<superscript>th</superscript> derivative of y(x). &kmplot; can only "
"interpret the derivative order as the number of primes following the "
"function name. To draw a sinusoidal curve, for example, you would use the "
"differential equation y'' = &minus; y."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:133
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"However, a differential equation on its own isn't enough to determine a "
"plot. Each curve in the diagram is generated by a combination of the "
"differential equation and the initial conditions. You can edit the initial "
"conditions by clicking on the <guilabel>Initial Conditions</guilabel> tab "
"when a differential equation is selected. The number of columns provided for "
"editing the initial conditions is dependent on the order of the differential "
"equation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: using.docbook:139
#, no-c-format
msgid "Step"
msgstr "Pas"

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:141
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The step value in the precision box is used in numerically solving the "
"differential equation (using the Runge Kutta method). Its value is the "
"maximum step size used; a smaller step size may be used if part of the "
"differential plot is zoomed in close enough."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:151
#, no-c-format
msgid "Combining Functions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:152
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Functions can be combined to produce new ones. Simply enter the functions "
"after the equals sign in an expression as if the functions were variables. "
"For example, if you have defined functions f(x) and g(x), you can plot the "
"sum of f and g with:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: using.docbook:156
#, no-c-format
msgid "<userinput>sum(x) = f(x) + g(x)</userinput>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid "Changing the appearance of functions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:163
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To change the appearance of a function's graph on the main plot window, "
"select the function in the <guilabel>Function Editor</guilabel> sidebar. You "
"can change the plot's line width, color and many other aspects by clicking "
"on the <guilabel>Appearance</guilabel> button at the bottom."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:168
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you are editing a Cartesian function, the function editor will have three "
"tabs. In the first one you specify the equation of the function. The "
"<guilabel>Derivatives</guilabel> tab lets you draw the first and second "
"derivative to the function. With the <guilabel>Integral</guilabel> tab you "
"can draw the integral of the function."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using.docbook:177
#, no-c-format
msgid "Popup menu"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:179
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When right-clicking on a plot function or a single-point parametric plot "
"function a popup menu will appear. In the menu there are three items "
"available:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenuitem
#: using.docbook:184
#, no-c-format
msgid "Hide"
msgstr "Amagar"

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:187
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Hides the selected graph. Other plots of the graph's function will still be "
"shown."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenuitem
#: using.docbook:192
#, no-c-format
msgid "Remove"
msgstr "Suprimir"

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:195
#, no-c-format
msgid "Removes the function. All its graphs will disappear."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenuitem
#: using.docbook:200
#, no-c-format
msgid "Edit"
msgstr "Edicion"

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:203
#, no-c-format
msgid "Selects the function in the <guilabel>Function Editor</guilabel> for editing."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:208
#, no-c-format
msgid "Depending on the plot type, there will also be up to four tools available:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenuitem
#: using.docbook:212
#, no-c-format
msgid "Get y-Value"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:215
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Opens a dialog in which you can find the y-value corresponding to a specific "
"x-value. The selected graph will be highlighted in the dialog. Enter an x "
"value in the <guilabel>X:</guilabel> box, and hit Enter. The corresponding y "
"will be automatically calculated and shown underneath."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenuitem
#: using.docbook:224
#, no-c-format
msgid "Search for Minimum Value"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:227
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Find the minimum value of the graph in a specified range. The selected graph "
"will be highlighted in the dialog that appears. Enter the lower and upper "
"boundaries of the region in which you want to search for a minimum."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:233
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note: You can also tell the plot to visually show the extreme points via the "
"plot's <guilabel>Appearance</guilabel> dialog, accessible via the Function "
"Editor."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenuitem
#: using.docbook:240
#, no-c-format
msgid "Search for Maximum Value"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:243
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is the same as <guimenuitem>Search for Minimum Value</guimenuitem> "
"above, but finds the maximum value instead of the minimum value."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenuitem
#: using.docbook:249
#, no-c-format
msgid "Calculate Integral"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using.docbook:252
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Select the x-values for the graph in the new dialog that appears. Calulates "
"the integral and draws the area between the graph and the x-axis in the "
"selected range in the color of the graph."
msgstr ""

